## Summary
Fixes #76233.

Approved exec follow-up turns for run-mode subagents can now keep an already materialized bundle MCP runtime alive until their active lease is released. Runtime retirement still starts immediately, blocks new leases, and global shutdown can still force disposal. A force disposal can also preempt an earlier normal disposal that is waiting on a lease.

## Root cause
`src/agents/agent-bundle-mcp-runtime.ts:1038` disposed a session runtime immediately even when `materializeBundleMcpToolsForRun` already held an active lease:

```before
async dispose() {
  if (disposed) {
    return;
  }
  disposed = true;
  catalog = null;
  catalogInFlight = undefined;
  const sessionsToClose = Array.from(sessions.values());
  sessions.clear();
  await Promise.allSettled(sessionsToClose.map((session) => disposeSession(session)));
}
```

That let subagent cleanup retire the bundle MCP runtime while a late approved follow-up turn was between runtime lookup and tool materialization, producing `bundle-mcp runtime disposed for session ...`.

## Fix
`src/agents/agent-bundle-mcp-runtime.ts:580` now tracks retirement start, waits for active leases before normal disposal, and rejects new leases once retirement begins:

```after
acquireLease() {
  failIfDisposing();
  activeLeases += 1;
  let released = false;
  return () => {
    if (released) {
      return;
    }
    released = true;
    activeLeases = Math.max(0, activeLeases - 1);
    resolveLeaseDrainers();
  };
}
```

`dispose({ force: true })` remains available for `disposeAll()` so process shutdown does not wait on live run leases, and a later force call wakes an existing normal disposal wait.

## Why this is the right boundary
The race is at the session MCP runtime ownership boundary, not in the exec follow-up text path. Materialized bundle MCP tools already acquire and release runtime leases; this change makes runtime retirement honor that existing ownership contract. The idle sweeper already skips active leases, and global teardown keeps forceful behavior through the explicit force option.

